<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
  <!--
    The controls a pointer drives that are not part of the keyboard picture or the editor's own
    chrome: the button roles, the two that carry a `selected` state of their own, and the field
    types with exactly one appearance in the app.

    Everything below is a *bridge*, not paint: each maps one class — or, for a type that has no
    competing variant, the type itself — onto a ControlTheme in Themes/ControlThemes/, which owns
    the template and the whole state matrix. Views keep writing `Classes="primaryAction"`, so the
    class stays the authoring API and nothing here has to know what the template looks like.

    That indirection is the point. These styles used to paint a button by reaching into Fluent's
    template for the content presenter, because Fluent's own `:pointerover` / `:pressed` /
    `:disabled` setters target it and a Background set on the Button itself would lose to them. A
    control theme that writes its own template has no such problem: its Border template-binds the
    Background, so a value set on the control wins. Nothing in Styles/ may name a template part
    again — see Themes/ControlThemes/Shared.axaml.

    A bridge sets the theme and, wherever it can, nothing else: a Style setter outranks a
    ControlTheme setter, so every extra property here is one the theme no longer decides.
  -->

  <!-- The one button of a surface that commits something: the editor's Save, a panel's Ok, Assign,
       a card's Configure. Accent, because accent means "you changed this" and this is the button
       that keeps it. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.primaryAction">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource PrimaryActionButton}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The bordered neutral button, and the default for anything that is not the committing action:
       Home, Scan for v-Drive, Cancel, Retry access. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.secondary">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource SecondaryButton}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The green-tinted Eject on a device card — the one place the app releases a drive. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.eject">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource EjectButton}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The red-tinted Discard in the leave-with-unsaved modal. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.discard">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource DiscardButton}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The key inspector's `● Record`: the button that arms capture so the next physical keypress
       becomes the assignment (the Remap panel's, and the Tap and Hold panel's two).

       It shares `DiscardButton`'s theme rather than adding a 28th, because it is the same red-tinted
       role and docs/design/handoff.md:82 says so outright — that hue is "also the Record ● color".
       Discard and Record are the app's two red buttons, and red means the same thing in both: this
       one is not the safe path. The `●` itself is drawn as an Ellipse at the call site (neither
       embedded IBM Plex family carries U+25CF), on the `recordDot` role in Styles/Editor.axaml.

       Only the theme is set here. A Setter in a Style outranks a ControlTheme setter, so a second
       property would be one the theme no longer decides. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.recordAction">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource DiscardButton}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The token picker's `All · Letters · Nav · Media · Mouse · Hotkeys · Recent` row (mockups 1e
       and 2a). `FilterChip` was written with the rest of the control-theme layer in #86 and left
       with no bridge and no call site, as substrate for exactly this; this is the bridge, and the
       key inspector's Remap panel is the call site.

       `Classes.selected` drives the chosen chip's face, which is the theme's own — one chip of the
       row is live at a time and the picker keeps the flags in step. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.filterChip">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource FilterChip}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- Chromeless until the pointer arrives: the small actions that must not compete with the
       button beside them. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.ghost">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource GhostButton}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- Accent text inside a sentence, no chrome. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.link">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource LinkButton}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- A full-width flat button that reads as a list entry: a macro in the profile's list, a
       recorded step. `Classes.selected` drives its selected face. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.rowButton">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource RowButton}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- One of a small set, exactly one of which is live: the macro-slot picker. The active pill is
       a raised neutral face rather than the accent — the accent means selection-of-a-thing-you-are-
       editing, and a slot you are merely looking at is not that. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.navPill">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource NavPill}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- A standalone two-state segment: the macro co-triggers, each independently on, and the pedal
       editor's Single Action / Macro latch. A Button driven by `Classes.selected` rather than a
       ListBoxItem, because none of these is one-of-a-selection. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.toggleSegment">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource ToggleSegment}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- One lighting zone (specs/07-lighting.md §4): a small chip in a wrapping row.

       IT POINTS AT `ToggleSegment` RATHER THAN AT A THEME OF ITS OWN, because since issue #124 a
       zone is exactly the shape that theme was written for: an independent latch driven by
       `Classes.selected`, not one-of-a-selection. A zone used to paint on the spot and so had no
       selected state to show — the class set padding, a margin and a cursor, and nothing else, which
       left the button on Fluent's own template with no way to say "these keys are the ones you
       picked". Now that a click selects and Apply commits, the chip needs the same neutral-off /
       accent-on ramp the macro co-triggers and the pedal latch wear, and it needs it to mean the
       same thing. Padding and cursor come from the theme (BaseButton's `PaddingButton` and `Hand`
       are the very values this style used to restate).

       The MARGIN stays here, and it is the one property this bridge is entitled to keep: it is the
       gap between chips in the WrapPanel that hosts them — a fact about the row, not about the
       control's appearance — so `ToggleSegment` decides nothing less for setting it. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.zoneButton">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource ToggleSegment}" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,8,8" />
  </Style>

  <!-- ===== Fields ============================================================================
       Three types with exactly one appearance each in this app, so the bridge is the type itself:
       inventing a class to carry a theme every instance wants would only be a second name for the
       same thing. A view that ever needs a second slider (or check box, or drop-down) adds a class
       then, and this style stays the default. The fourth field, the setting switch, is a class —
       see its own note below.

       There is deliberately no blanket `ListBoxItem` style any more. One used to sit here for the
       Search Keys picker's compact rows and leaked onto every list in the app, including the
       segmented control's — and because an app style outranks a ControlTheme setter, a segment's
       padding came out 8,4 instead of the theme's 11,4. The picker names
       `ItemContainerTheme="{StaticResource SelectableListRow}"` instead, which is the same rhythm
       scoped to the one list that asked for it. -->
  <Style Selector="Slider">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource Slider}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="CheckBox">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource CheckBox}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="ComboBox">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource ComboBox}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The on/off rows of the device-settings section. A CLASS rather than the type, unlike the
       three above, and for the reason the by-type rule itself gives: `ToggleButton` is a primitive
       that other things are built out of, not a widget with one natural appearance, so a latch or a
       segment written as a bare ToggleButton later must not silently inherit a pill and a knob.
       (The theme targets ToggleButton rather than ToggleSwitch because ToggleSwitch declares a
       required template part with the prefix contract 2 bans — see
       Themes/ControlThemes/Fields.axaml.) -->
  <Style Selector="ToggleButton.settingSwitch">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource SettingSwitch}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The app's query box: the token picker's, in the key inspector rail and in the macro-insertion
       modal alike. Sans, not mono: it holds what the user is looking for, never a value the config
       file carries — which is exactly the line `TextBox.monoValue` in Styles/Text.axaml sits on the
       other side of. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBox.searchField">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource SearchField}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The app's one indeterminate bar: the 3px strip across the bottom of a dashboard card while a
       detection pass is in flight (docs/design/mockups.md §2e). Fluent gives it no palette key of
       its own, so the three colours are set here; the card sets the *geometry* on the control
       itself — a Height of 3, Fluent's MinHeight cleared, and no radius — because those are
       properties on the ProgressBar rather than parts of its template, which is what keeps this a
       paint style instead of a fourteenth ControlTheme. -->
  <Style Selector="ProgressBar">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceInsetBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
  </Style>
</Styles>
